<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gorgan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gurgan, Jurjan, Astarabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gorgan, historically Gurgan or Jurjan, is a city and region on the south-eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a>. In the early Islamic period it was a prosperous province and the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a>, which contested it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>. The nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonbad-e-qabus-tower">Gonbad-e Qabus</a>, the tomb tower of the Ziyarid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus">Qabus</a>, is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gorgan.jpg" alt="Gorgan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: mohammadmoheimany · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masjed_jame_gorgan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The present city of Gorgan is the capital of Golestan province and lies about 400 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and some 30 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. North of it stretches the wide Dasht-e Gorgan, the Plain of Gorgan. The city has hot, humid summers and cool, wet winters, with about 584 millimetres of rain a year, while the Gorgan valley has a semi-arid climate. Some 150 km to the east is the Golestan National Park.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>More than 50 archaeological sites lie on the Gorgan Plain, among them Tureng Tepe and Shah Tepe, with remains from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and Chalcolithic periods. According to the Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, Zadracarta was the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a> and the site of its royal palace. Hyrcania became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> or his successor Cambyses. The Great Wall of Gorgan was built in the Parthian and Sasanian periods, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> the name Gurgan was used for a city, a provincial capital and a province.</p><p>The region kept its independence as a Zoroastrian state even after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Arab conquest of Persia</a> in the 7th century. In 1210 the city was invaded and sacked by the army of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakarids">Mkhargrdzeli</a> brothers. Old Gorgan was destroyed during the Mongol invasion of the 13th century, and the centre of the region moved to Astarabad, which is the city now called Gorgan. Astarabad was an important political and religious city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2016 counted 350,676 people in the city, against 269,226 in 2006. It has several universities, among them Golestan University and Gorgan University. Among the people the city counts as its own are the 11th-century poet Fakhroddin Asaad Gorgani, composer of Vis and Ramin, the 11th-century grammarian Abd al-Qahir al-Jurjani, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a>, Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1789 to 1797.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gorgan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Adud al-Dawla's campaigns were the most successful: in 977–978 he defeated his cousin Bakhtiyar, who was killed at the battle of Qasr al-Jiss in 978, and in 979 he took Mosul from the Hamdanids, while his brother Mu'ayyad al-Dawla drove the Ziyarid ruler Qabus out of <b>Gurgan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qajars were a Turkmen tribe settled since Safavid times around <b>Astarabad</b>, south-east of the Caspian Sea, and their chiefs had competed for power in the turmoil that followed the fall of the Safavids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mardavij ibn Ziyar, a Gilite commander who had served Daylamite warlords, seized Tabaristan and <b>Gorgan</b> and then the Jibal around 931.</blockquote><a href="/state/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
